>SunOS 4.1.2 is decidedly NOT SVR4 or a superset, for better or worse.

But it _is_ more or less very close to a SVR4 superset, even if you
think of this as meaning that it is not at all a SVR4 superset.

What I said can have the (intended) interpretation that anything which
is more or less a superset of SVR4 is OK, and also anything which is
'very close' to being that is OK too, where 'very close' is taken to
mean at least as close as SunOS 4.1.2. By this criteria, it is apparent
that for example BSD4.x is _not_ OK since BSD4.4 is not a commercially
supported standard or BSD4.<=3 which _is_ essentially supported commercially
in a somewhat standard way, is not close enough to being a superset of
SVR4 (lacking the virtual memory approach of SunOs). ODT, some versions
of HP-UX and AIX are not OK, and obviously things like Coherent, Xenix,
OS/2 2.0 and (probably) Windows NT are right out.


