I'm not 100% sure that I got this correctly, but this is what I
was told is the origin of DCE rpc.

They took 1000 people from the marketing departments of all the
finest Unix/workstation companies around the world and put them
into a room.  In the room was a table with 1000 copies of every
computer buzzword dictionary book that has ever been published.
Next to that pile there was a pile of blank pads of paper.  I'm
told there were about 3 pads for every person in the room.  The
chalkboard at the front of the room had the assignment:  "Write
down every feature you can think an rpc system should have.  If
you think of any nifty new features that might possibly impress
a technically illiterate corporate CEO, put an asterisk next to
that line."

About two days later the people left the room, possibly to find
food, or maybe they just discovered something that other people
discovered years ago (that sitting around with marketing people
is boring).  All of them had the most impressive grins on their
faces.

When the final person had left the room, all that was remaining
was the table, the chalk board, a pile of papers.  The top page
read: "DCE Specification."

As I said before, I can't prove this, it's just what I was told.

...but it was from a reliable source!

Tom
